# Rank 1006 / CVE-2026-9298: omec-project/amf NGAP crash on malformed input.
# The advisory (VulDB GHSA-h3w8-h79v-64cv, CWE-119, PathSwitchRequest
# handler) describes remotely
# triggerable memory corruption; in Go this is a panic (nil-pointer dereference
# or index out of range) that crashes the AMF. Fix 34bc6724ac (PR #666) is a
# broad hardening pass: it adds nil-checks on decoded NGAP information elements
# across many handlers (RANUENGAPID / AMFUENGAPID in the PathSwitchRequest path,
# nil PDU / connection / RAN guards in ngap/dispatcher.go) plus length and digit
# validation in util.PlmnIdStringToModels, which sliced plmnId[:3] and
# plmnId[3:] with no length check and panics on a short string. The string
# reaches the slicer from a NAS-decoded mobile identity, so a taint route would
# also need a source on the third-party decode (nasConvert.SuciToString in
# github.com/omec-project/nas), which is outside this tree.
#
# ATTENTION, no precise generic detection; the precision cost is measured, not
# argued.
#
#  - Both fix axes leave the vulnerable construct in place on the fixed
#    revision: the slicer still runs plmnId[:3] and plmnId[3:] after validating
#    the length, and the handlers still read the decoded information element's
#    Value, now behind guarded helpers. Only a binding that also models the
#    added guard can separate the revisions.
#  - The guard is not expressible generically. A presence binding cannot match
#    "a numeric slice offset" (the regex matcher is rejected in presence
#    predicates, internal/bindings/compile_presence.go:268), so only enumerated
#    offset literals remain, which is the repro itself.
#  - The negated shape "fixed-offset slice with no len() in the function"
#    separates this pair 1 -> 0 and is unshippable: by textual count the
#    non-test Go standard library alone carries 1568 functions with a
#    fixed-offset slice expression, 605 of them with no len() call, and on this
#    tree the shape flags 9 functions on the vulnerable revision and 8 on the
#    fixed one. The collateral includes a slice of a fixed-size array parameter
#    (StmsiToGuti(buf [7]byte) in nas/nas_security/security.go), which cannot
#    go out of range at all.
#  - The nil-check axis is per-library and does not track the fix: 31 functions
#    in this tree read a decoded *NGAPID .Value, and the "unguarded in this
#    function" variant of that shape flags 3 functions on the vulnerable
#    revision and 2 on the fixed one, because the fix moves the reads into
#    guarded helpers while the flagged sites hold their guards elsewhere.
#  - Measured on the real checkout, 3 x --cache off per revision, --max-ram
#    6GB: both revisions report the same four rows with identical fingerprints
#    (VYQL-CFG-003 once, VYQL-VAL-036 three times, all on the OAM HTTP
#    handlers); the ngap/ + util/ + gmm/ subtree reports zero findings in the
#    full findings view; the review-flag view (120 flags) holds nothing on
#    ngap/handler.go, ngap/dispatcher.go or util/convert.go; and vyql match on
#    those files and on minimal repros of both axes reports no source, no sink
#    and no control label. No Go binding labels a decoded protocol field as a
#    source or a slice as a sink today.
#
# verify_current_expected: attention-precision-cost

test "rank1006: PlmnId string sliced at fixed offsets with no length check panics"
  lang go
  file util/convert.go
  reject VYQL-MEM-006
  code
  ```
  package util

  import "github.com/omec-project/openapi/models"

  func PlmnIdStringToModels(plmnId string) (plmnID models.PlmnId) {
      plmnID.Mcc = plmnId[:3]
      plmnID.Mnc = plmnId[3:]
      return
  }
  ```

test "rank1006: fix validates the PlmnId length and digits before slicing"
  lang go
  file util/convert.go
  reject VYQL-MEM-006
  code
  ```
  package util

  import (
      "fmt"

      "github.com/omec-project/openapi/models"
  )

  func PlmnIdStringToModels(plmnId string) (plmnID models.PlmnId, err error) {
      if len(plmnId) < 5 || len(plmnId) > 6 {
          return models.PlmnId{}, fmt.Errorf("invalid PLMN ID length %d", len(plmnId))
      }
      for _, digit := range plmnId {
          if digit < '0' || digit > '9' {
              return models.PlmnId{}, fmt.Errorf("invalid PLMN ID %q", plmnId)
          }
      }
      plmnID.Mcc = plmnId[:3]
      plmnID.Mnc = plmnId[3:]
      return plmnID, nil
  }
  ```
